<br class="ltx_break">hoshino@cc.nagoya-u.ac.jp
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Takahiro Katagiri
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Information Technology Center
<br class="ltx_break">Nagoya University
<br class="ltx_break">Aichi, Japan
<br class="ltx_break">katagiri@cc.nagoya-u.ac.jp
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose VibeCodeHPC, an automatic tuning system for HPC programs based on multi-agent LLMs for code generation. VibeCodeHPC tunes programs through multi-agent role allocation and iterative prompt refinement. We describe the system configuration with four roles: Project Manager (PM), System Engineer (SE), Programmer (PG), and Continuous Delivery (CD). We introduce dynamic agent deployment and activity monitoring functions to facilitate effective multi-agent collaboration. In our case study, we convert and optimize CPU-based matrix–matrix multiplication code written in C to GPU code using CUDA. The multi-agent configuration of VibeCodeHPC achieved higher-quality code generation per unit time compared to a solo-agent configuration. Additionally, the dynamic agent deployment and activity monitoring capabilities facilitated more effective identification of requirement violations and other issues.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>Code Generative AI, Auto-tuning, Vibe Coding, Multi AI Agents, Software Productivity

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S1.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">I-A</span> </span><span id="S1.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Background</span></h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In recent years, large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in code generation, automatically transforming natural language specifications into executable programs. Trained on massive corpora of source code and natural language text, these models have found applications in rapid prototyping, in code refactoring, and in automated testing. However, while LLM-based systems have achieved promising results in generating functionally correct code, their application to performance-critical domains such as High-Performance Computing (HPC) remains limited.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">HPC programming imposes strict requirements beyond functional correctness. Applications in this domain must achieve optimal execution time, minimize power consumption, and balance numerical accuracy with computational speed. Moreover, HPC software typically relies on parallel programming models – MPI, OpenMP, CUDA, and OpenACC – which requires appropriate strategy selection based on application characteristics and target execution environments. Meeting these demands requires not only correct code generation but also architecture-aware performance tuning and parallelization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the rapid progress of LLMs, several challenges hinder their effective use for HPC code generation:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Limited integration with auto-tuning</span>: Auto-tuning systematically explores parameter spaces to find optimal configurations—a proven approach for performance optimization. However, current LLM-based systems lack full integration with auto-tuning workflows, often generating code without performance optimization. The autonomous application of domain-specific knowledge, such as memory access optimization and communication design, remains limited.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lack of HPC-specific considerations</span>: Current code generation systems rarely address HPC-specific requirements including parallelization strategy selection, numerical accuracy trade-offs, and power efficiency. Notably, these systems cannot automatically select optimal parallelization methods for given workloads and environments.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Immature iterative improvement cycles</span>: Performance evaluation, feedback integration, and iterative refinement – essential for self-optimizing code generation – are not well established in existing systems.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S1.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">I-B</span> </span><span id="S1.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Vibe Coding and VibeCodeHPC</span></h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we apply the Vibe Coding paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> to performance tuning. Vibe Coding enables rapid prototyping through code generation and performance evaluation, even when specifications remain uncertain or incomplete.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Building on this paradigm, we propose <span id="S1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">VibeCodeHPC</span>, a novel code generation framework for HPC applications that tightly integrates auto-tuning into the generation and refinement process. This framework bridges the gap between LLM capabilities and HPC requirements through systematic performance optimization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S1.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">I-C</span> </span><span id="S1.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Aims and Contributions</span></h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Current LLM-based code generation systems have successfully interpreted natural language specifications and produced executable code. However, in performance-critical domains such as HPC, several challenges remain:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Auto-tuning integration</span>: We propose an iterative prompting mechanism incorporating performance feedback via auto-tuning, enabling performance-guided code refinement. Architecture-aware optimization is achieved through embedded auto-tuning parameter selection.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parallelization and accuracy control</span>: Our system standardizes code generation for MPI, OpenMP, CUDA, and OpenACC while incorporating user-defined trade-offs between numerical accuracy and computational speed.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-agent iterative refinement</span>: We design a multi-agent architecture where specialized AI agents handle distinct auto-tuning tasks, enabling continuous improvement of generated HPC code quality.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dynamic agent management</span>: Multi-agent systems require runtime agent deployment and activity monitoring for flexible processing and appropriate instruction dispatch. We propose implementation and prompt-engineering approaches to realize these capabilities.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By combining LLM-based code generation with auto-tuning, HPC-specific optimization, and multi-agent collaboration, VibeCodeHPC bridges the gap between functional correctness and peak performance in autonomous HPC code development.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S1.SS4.4" class="ltx_text">I-D</span> </span><span id="S1.SS4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Organization of the Paper</span></h3>

<div id="S1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section <a href="#S2" title="II VibeCodeHPC ‣ VibeCodeHPC: An Agent-Based Iterative Prompting Auto-Tuner for HPC Code Generation Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> presents VibeCodeHPC in detail. Section <a href="#S3" title="III Performance Evaluation ‣ VibeCodeHPC: An Agent-Based Iterative Prompting Auto-Tuner for HPC Code Generation Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a> evaluates the performance of the proposed methods. Section <a href="#S4" title="IV Related Work ‣ VibeCodeHPC: An Agent-Based Iterative Prompting Auto-Tuner for HPC Code Generation Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a> reviews related work and positions our contribution within the field. Finally, Section <a href="#S5" title="V Conclusion ‣ VibeCodeHPC: An Agent-Based Iterative Prompting Auto-Tuner for HPC Code Generation Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a> summarizes our findings and discusses future directions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">VibeCodeHPC</span></h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">System Overview</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose VibeCodeHPC, a code generation framework for HPC applications that integrates LLM-driven code synthesis with auto-tuning and AI-agent iterative refinement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">VibeCodeHPC adopts the Vibe Coding paradigm, which supports rapid prototyping and performance optimization even under uncertain or evolving specifications. In this approach, users provide requirements through prompts without formulating detailed specifications or engaging in manual code development. Detailed experiments are presented in Section <a href="#S3" title="III Performance Evaluation ‣ VibeCodeHPC: An Agent-Based Iterative Prompting Auto-Tuner for HPC Code Generation Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">VibeCodeHPC comprises three primary components:</p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM-based Code Generator</span>: Generates initial HPC code from natural language specifications, incorporating domain constraints such as numerical accuracy requirements, target parallel programming models (MPI, OpenMP, CUDA, OpenACC), and architecture-specific considerations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Auto-Tuning Engine</span>: Explores parameter spaces (thread counts, block sizes, tiling factors, communication strategies) using search algorithms including Bayesian optimization and genetic algorithms, collecting performance metrics for each configuration.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Iterative Refinement through Prompting with AI Agents</span>: This approach establishes a feedback loop in which performance evaluation results are fed back to the LLM, thereby guiding the iterative refinement of the generated code. Multiple specialized AI agents are responsible for sub-tasks such as performance data analysis, selection of parallelization strategies, and the evaluation of numerical accuracy trade-offs.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ II-A System Overview ‣ II VibeCodeHPC ‣ VibeCodeHPC: An Agent-Based Iterative Prompting Auto-Tuner for HPC Code Generation Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> illustrates the VibeCodeHPC architecture, showing interactions between the LLM and AI agents with the auto-tuning engine and multi-agent feedback. User-provided requirements are processed starting from the Experimental Planning AG (3) in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ II-A System Overview ‣ II VibeCodeHPC ‣ VibeCodeHPC: An Agent-Based Iterative Prompting Auto-Tuner for HPC Code Generation Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.00031v1/fig1.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/293;" width="476" height="293" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 1: </span>A conceptual design of VibeCodeHPC.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ II-A System Overview ‣ II VibeCodeHPC ‣ VibeCodeHPC: An Agent-Based Iterative Prompting Auto-Tuner for HPC Code Generation Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we assign the following five AI agents (AGs), each controlled by an LLM with user-specified execution scripts:</p>
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Candidate Generation AG</span>: Generates code variants through mutation and manages the candidate pool. Uses benchmark results (execution time, numerical accuracy) to guide subsequent generation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Candidate Selection AG</span>: Selects promising candidates from the generated code pool. Defines benchmarking criteria and uses performance evaluation results for selection decisions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experimental Planning AG</span>: Creates code generation plans based on user requirements and produces necessary execution scripts. Incorporates selected candidates to enhance generation capabilities and triggers fine-tuning when sufficient code samples accumulate.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Code Generation AG</span>: Generates code following experimental plans using strategies including one-shot, multi-shot, and chain-of-thought approaches. Employs retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) to switch between commercial and local LLMs. Maintains generation history for the Benchmark/Code Verification AG.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmark/Code Verification AG</span>: Verifies and benchmarks generated code through compilation and execution. Collects metrics including execution time, numerical accuracy, and script-specified benchmarks. Returns aggregated results to the Candidate Generation AG.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Iterative Prompting Workflow</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The VibeCodeHPC workflow consists of five iterative steps:</p>
<ol id="S2.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Specification Input</span>: Users provide natural language specifications including functional requirements, target hardware, performance goals, and optional constraints (acceptable accuracy loss, energy budget) through the Experimental Planning AG (AG3).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Initial Code Generation</span>: The Code Generation AG (AG4) produces an initial implementation using specified parallelization models. When no model is specified, the system proposes candidates based on historical data and hardware characteristics.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Performance Evaluation</span>: The Benchmark/Code Verification AG (AG5) executes generated code on the target system, systematically exploring parameter spaces and collecting metrics including execution time, energy consumption, and accuracy.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I3.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Feedback Integration</span>: The Candidate Generation AG (AG1) analyzes performance data through specialized agents, identifies bottlenecks (memory-bound kernels, load imbalance), and constructs refined prompts with recommendations for alternative strategies, loop transformations, or numerical approximations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I3.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I3.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Iterative Refinement</span>: The entire loop in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ II-A System Overview ‣ II VibeCodeHPC ‣ VibeCodeHPC: An Agent-Based Iterative Prompting Auto-Tuner for HPC Code Generation Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> updates code based on feedback, repeating until performance meets user thresholds or improvement rates fall below specified limits.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">II-C</span> </span><span id="S2.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">AI Agent Collaboration</span></h3>

<section id="S2.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S2.SS3.SSS1.4" class="ltx_text">II-C</span>1 </span>Overview</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">VibeCodeHPC employs a multi-agent architecture with specialized agents for distinct optimization tasks:</p>
<ul id="S2.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Code Generation Agent</span>: Produces performant HPC code from natural language prompts.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Performance Analysis Agent</span>: Identifies bottlenecks and estimates optimization impact.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I4.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parallelization Strategy Agent</span>: Selects and configures parallel programming models (MPI, OpenMP, CUDA, OpenACC) based on workload and hardware characteristics.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I4.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accuracy-Performance Trade-off Agent</span>: Balances numerical precision with execution speed according to user constraints.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I4.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I4.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I4.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I4.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Auto-Tuning Orchestration Agent</span>: Manages search algorithms and schedules parameter tuning experiments.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This agent-based design enables concurrent processing of auto-tuning tasks and facilitates integration of domain-specific optimization modules without system-wide modifications.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS4.4" class="ltx_text">II-D</span> </span><span id="S2.SS4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Dynamic Inter-Agent Monitoring</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Effective multi-agent collaboration requires dynamic information sharing regarding agent activity status (e.g., token usage), including token usage. Additionally, agent deployment must support runtime reallocation, enabling dynamic scaling of agents with identical objectives based on situational demands.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Existing multi-agent implementations (e.g., Tmux Multi-agent Communication Demo<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
            https://github.com/nishimoto265/Claude-Code-Communication/blob/main/README-en.md</span></span></span>) support only fixed allocation at startup, lacking deployment flexibility. To address this limitation, VibeCodeHPC implements dynamic agent deployment, as illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ II-D Dynamic Inter-Agent Monitoring ‣ II VibeCodeHPC ‣ VibeCodeHPC: An Agent-Based Iterative Prompting Auto-Tuner for HPC Code Generation Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ II-D Dynamic Inter-Agent Monitoring ‣ II VibeCodeHPC ‣ VibeCodeHPC: An Agent-Based Iterative Prompting Auto-Tuner for HPC Code Generation Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the PM agent launches via the launch script. Within the Common Processing Flow, hook functions control agent behaviors, preventing polling-type agents from entering idle states.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The Data Management component obtains agent IDs and session information, creating a database to store per-agent information including token consumption status. From this activity database, a Context Usage Report is generated periodically. This report enables users to monitor system status and allows the management agent to assess subordinate agent activity and issue appropriate instructions. Specifically, it provides data for evaluating memory compaction performed by the code generation agents’ auto-compact feature (Section <a href="#S3" title="III Performance Evaluation ‣ VibeCodeHPC: An Agent-Based Iterative Prompting Auto-Tuner for HPC Code Generation Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">These mechanisms enable dynamic agent allocation, allowing the management agent to launch subordinate agents as needed, forming the core of VibeCodeHPC’s distinctive multi-agent functionality.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.00031v1/fig2.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/290;" width="476" height="290" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 2: </span>Dynamic agent allocation and status monitoring in VibeCodeHPC.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS5.4" class="ltx_text">II-E</span> </span><span id="S2.SS5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Advantages over Conventional Approaches</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to traditional code generation systems and standalone auto-tuning frameworks, VibeCodeHPC offers several key advantages:</p>
<ul id="S2.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Integrated generation and tuning</span>: Performance optimization is embedded from the initial code generation stage through tight coupling.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multiple parallelization model support</span>: Multiple parallelization models are supported within a unified refinement loop.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I5.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Flexible performance trade-offs</span>: User-defined constraints explicitly control the balance between numerical accuracy and computational speed.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Performance Evaluation</span></h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Experimental Environments</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conducted experiments using the Supercomputer “Flow” Type II Subsystem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> at the Information Technology Center, Nagoya University.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The system specifications include: two Intel Xeon Gold 6230 CPUs (20 cores, 2.10-3.90 GHz); four NVIDIA Tesla V100 GPUs (2560 FP64 cores, up to 1530 MHz); 384 GiB DDR4 main memory (2933 MHz); and 32 GiB HBM2 device memory per GPU. The theoretical peak performance reaches 33.888 TFLOPS in double precision (1.344 TFLOPS per CPU socket, 7.8 TFLOPS per GPU). Memory bandwidth comprises 281.5 GB/s for main memory and 900 GB/s per GPU for device memory. GPU interconnection uses NVLINK2 (50 GB/s bidirectional), while CPU-GPU connection employs PCI-Express 3.0 (x16).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The underlying Claude Code is installed on a local PC, which also serves as the launch point for the multi-agent system. Code generation is performed on this PC, and the resulting code is transferred to the supercomputer via SSH. Job submission to the supercomputer is likewise initiated from the local PC. Notably, most of this workflow is implemented through script engineering within Claude Code. For efficient management of SSH and SFTP sessions, we employed Desktop Commander MCP<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
            https://github.com/wonderwhy-er/DesktopCommanderMCP</span></span></span>, which is particularly recommended for supercomputers that require two-factor authentication.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">VibeCodeHPC is implemented with the following:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">VibeCodeHPC-jp v0.6.10<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                    <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
                    
                    
                  https://github.com/Katagiri-Hoshino-Lab/VibeCodeHPC-jp/releases/tag/v0.6.10</span></span></span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Windows 11 (local PC)</p>
<ul id="S3.I1.i2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">WSL version: 2.4.13.0</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
