</div>
<div id="lstnumberx21" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx21.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">PG1</span><span id="lstnumberx21.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">.1</span><span id="lstnumberx21.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">:</span><span id="lstnumberx21.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx21.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_string ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">"[PM]<span id="lstnumberx21.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>Emergency<span id="lstnumberx21.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>stop!<span id="lstnumberx21.5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>v1.3.0<span id="lstnumberx21.5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>violates<span id="lstnumberx21.5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>the<span id="lstnumberx21.5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>requirements<span id="lstnumberx21.5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>due<span id="lstnumberx21.5.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>to<span id="lstnumberx21.5.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>cuBLAS<span id="lstnumberx21.5.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>usage.<span id="lstnumberx21.5.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>Please<span id="lstnumberx21.5.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>optimize<span id="lstnumberx21.5.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>with<span id="lstnumberx21.5.14" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>an<span id="lstnumberx21.5.15" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>in-house<span id="lstnumberx21.5.16" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>implementation<span id="lstnumberx21.5.17" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>only.<span id="lstnumberx21.5.18" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>The<span id="lstnumberx21.5.19" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>goal<span id="lstnumberx21.5.20" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>is<span id="lstnumberx21.5.21" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>to<span id="lstnumberx21.5.22" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>achieve<span id="lstnumberx21.5.23" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>60%<span id="lstnumberx21.5.24" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>without<span id="lstnumberx21.5.25" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>cuBLAS."</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx22" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx22.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">SE1:</span><span id="lstnumberx22.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx22.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_string ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">"[PM]<span id="lstnumberx22.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>v1.3.0<span id="lstnumberx22.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>is<span id="lstnumberx22.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>invalid.<span id="lstnumberx22.3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>cuBLAS<span id="lstnumberx22.3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>usage<span id="lstnumberx22.3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>is<span id="lstnumberx22.3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>a<span id="lstnumberx22.3.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>requirements<span id="lstnumberx22.3.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>violation.<span id="lstnumberx22.3.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>Please<span id="lstnumberx22.3.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>exclude<span id="lstnumberx22.3.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>it<span id="lstnumberx22.3.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>from<span id="lstnumberx22.3.14" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>the<span id="lstnumberx22.3.15" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>graph.<span id="lstnumberx22.3.16" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>The<span id="lstnumberx22.3.17" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>valid<span id="lstnumberx22.3.18" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>highest<span id="lstnumberx22.3.19" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>performance<span id="lstnumberx22.3.20" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>is<span id="lstnumberx22.3.21" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>28.02%<span id="lstnumberx22.3.22" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>in<span id="lstnumberx22.3.23" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>v1.2.1."</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx23" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx23.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;">PM:</span><span id="lstnumberx23.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx23.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_string ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">"[SE1]<span id="lstnumberx23.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>Understood.<span id="lstnumberx23.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>I<span id="lstnumberx23.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>will<span id="lstnumberx23.3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>treat<span id="lstnumberx23.3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>v1.3.0<span id="lstnumberx23.3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>as<span id="lstnumberx23.3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>invalid.<span id="lstnumberx23.3.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>The<span id="lstnumberx23.3.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>valid<span id="lstnumberx23.3.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>highest<span id="lstnumberx23.3.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>performance<span id="lstnumberx23.3.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>is<span id="lstnumberx23.3.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>28.02%<span id="lstnumberx23.3.14" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>in<span id="lstnumberx23.3.15" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>v1.2.1.<span id="lstnumberx23.3.16" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>I<span id="lstnumberx23.3.17" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>will<span id="lstnumberx23.3.18" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>update<span id="lstnumberx23.3.19" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>the<span id="lstnumberx23.3.20" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>graph<span id="lstnumberx23.3.21" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>accordingly."</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS5.SSS2.4" class="ltx_text">III-E</span>2 </span>Generated Codes</h4>

<div id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Regarding the v1.4.0 CUDA optimization code (43.14% of theoretical performance) generated by the multi-agents, the following techniques were automatically incorporated:</p>
<ol id="S3.I9" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I9.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I9.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I9.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">double buffering combined with read-only cache optimization to hide the access latency to the L2 cache;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I9.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I9.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I9.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">utilization of the read-only texture cache through the __ldg (read-only data cache) instruction;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I9.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I9.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I9.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">blocking scheme: BLOCK_M/N = 64, BLOCK_K = 16.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This implementation can be regarded as a valid one in CUDA. The fact that such code can be automatically generated is an interesting case, despite being the optimization of a simple matrix–matrix multiplication code, which is assumed to have abundant training data.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS5.SSS3.4" class="ltx_text">III-E</span>3 </span>Summary</h4>

<div id="S3.SS5.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, the success of the multi-agent approach can be attributed to several factors. These include stepwise optimization, in which the implementation gradually progressed from a basic version to advanced optimizations; parallel development, where multiple programmers simultaneously explored different approaches; rapid feedback, enabling early detection of failures and timely course correction; and budget management, which allowed efficient job execution at low cost.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, there remains considerable room for improvement. The achieved performance was only 43.14%, compared to the target of 60%. Time constraints also limited the opportunity to explore additional optimization methods. Furthermore, aggressive optimization attempts, such as those implemented in version 1.5.0, resulted in accuracy issues.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Related Work</span></h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM-Based Code Generation</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) including GPT-4, LLaMA 3, and Code LLaMA have significantly advanced automatic code generation. These models have demonstrated capabilities in producing syntactically correct and semantically meaningful code from natural language descriptions, with applications in software prototyping, automated bug fixing, and code refactoring. Chen et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> showed that models trained on large-scale code corpora can generate effective solutions for programming contest problems. Austin et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> and Li et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> introduced prompt engineering and in-context learning strategies to improve code correctness and robustness. However, these studies primarily focus on general-purpose programming tasks without addressing HPC-specific performance constraints.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">HPC Code Generation and Optimization</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">HPC code generation traditionally relies on domain experts to manually implement performance-critical kernels using parallel programming models such as MPI, OpenMP, CUDA, and OpenACC. Compiler-assisted approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> have aimed to automate parts of this process by applying loop transformations, memory layout optimizations, and vectorization. More recent studies have investigated domain-specific languages (DSLs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> to generate optimized HPC kernels from high-level specifications. While these methods can yield high performance, they typically require explicit user annotations or DSL expertise, limiting their accessibility. The integration of LLMs into HPC code generation remains largely unexplored, especially in conjunction with automated performance tuning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">AT and Iterative Refinement</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">AT has been widely adopted in HPC to search parameter spaces and identify optimal configurations for target architectures. Frameworks such as ATLAS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, OpenTuner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, and GPTune <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> automate this process, often leveraging search algorithms like Bayesian optimization, genetic algorithms, or multi-armed bandits. Additionally, iterative refinement strategies—where code is repeatedly modified based on performance feedback—have been explored in machine learning-guided compilation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> . While these approaches are effective in optimizing existing code, their integration with LLM-driven code synthesis is still in its infancy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS4.4" class="ltx_text">IV-D</span> </span><span id="S4.SS4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Iterative Prompt in LLM-based Code Generation</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent studies have explored iterative prompting in code generation AI. DiCuffa et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> used the DevGPT dataset to demonstrate that structural prompt patterns enable high-quality outputs with fewer iterations. Sorokin et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> introduced an iterative self-learning loop with re-ranking models and approximate policy optimization, achieving performance surpassing GPT-4. Ye et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> proposed Prochemy, which automatically refines initial prompts based on execution results, enhancing performance in code translation and HumanEval tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, iteration can introduce vulnerabilities. Shukla et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> reported a 37.6% increase in critical vulnerabilities after five iterations, highlighting the need for human oversight. From a structural perspective, Ren et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> proposed Knowledge-driven Prompt Chaining (KPC) for exception handling, while Li et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> introduced Structured CoT, improving Pass@1 accuracy by up to 13.79% on HumanEval. Ridnik et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> demonstrated that test-driven, multi-stage iterative flows improved Pass@5 accuracy from 19% to 44%.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Wei et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> investigated CUDA code optimization using multi-agent systems without additional training. However, the role-sharing approach proposed in our study has not been evaluated for cross-language optimization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These studies constitute methodological investigations into iterative prompting. Research on AI-agent collaboration paradigms, as exemplified by VibeCodeHPC, remains nascent.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS5.4" class="ltx_text">IV-E</span> </span><span id="S4.SS5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Summary</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Existing research in LLM-based code generation has achieved significant progress in producing functionally correct programs, but it rarely addresses HPC-specific performance optimization. On the other hand, the HPC community has developed powerful auto-tuning and compiler-based optimization techniques, yet these methods often lack the flexibility and adaptability of LLM-based code synthesis. Bridging these domains—by integrating LLM-driven generation, HPC performance tuning, and iterative improvement—remains an open challenge, which our proposed VibeCodeHPC framework seeks to address.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Conclusion</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We presented VibeCodeHPC, a novel LLM-driven framework for generating and optimizing HPC code through tight integration of auto-tuning and iterative refinement. Unlike conventional code generation systems focusing primarily on functional correctness, VibeCodeHPC explicitly incorporates HPC-specific concerns including parallelization strategy selection, numerical accuracy–performance trade-offs, and architecture-aware optimization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our multi-agent architecture coordinates specialized AI agents to analyze performance metrics, explore parameter spaces, and refine generated code in a closed feedback loop. Experimental results demonstrate substantial performance improvements while preserving the flexibility of natural language–driven code synthesis. The framework features dynamic multi-agent deployment and activity monitoring, where aggregated reports enable management agents to issue appropriate instructions to subordinate agents.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond addressing current limitations in LLM-based HPC code generation, VibeCodeHPC enables rapid performance prototyping under uncertain or evolving specifications, allowing both domain experts and non-experts to efficiently explore high-performance solutions.
Future work will extend the framework to:</p>
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Integrate dynamic workload adaptation for runtime performance optimization</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Incorporate reinforcement learning for efficient auto-tuning parameter exploration</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Support emerging heterogeneous computing platforms including exascale and quantum-accelerated systems</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">VibeCodeHPC represents a significant advancement toward bridging AI-assisted programming and expert-level HPC performance tuning, fostering a new generation of performance-aware, AI-driven development tools.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The iterative prompting approach of VibeCodeHPC can be implemented within existing auto-tuning frameworks, languages, and tools <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. Integrating the proposed approach into auto-tuning systems represents progress toward broader generalization. Extending the auto-tuning framework for compatibility with diverse AI-based code generation platforms remains a key challenge for future research. The VibeCodeHPC code is available<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
            
            
          https://github.com/Katagiri-Hoshino-Lab/VibeCodeHPC-jp</span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgment</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work was supported by the Joint Usage/Research Center for Interdisciplinary Large-scale Information Infrastructures (JHPCN) and the High-Performance Computing Infrastructure (HPCI) under project number jh250015. In addition, this research was funded by JSPS KAKENHI Grants JP23K11126 and JP24K02945.</p>
</div>
</section>
